.TH HACKRFDUPLEX-CHECK 1 "2026-11-10" "hackrfduplex 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hackrfduplex-check \- check a HackRF One pair through the
hackrfduplex SoapySDR module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hackrfduplex-check
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hackrfduplex-check
opens two HackRF One boards as one full-duplex SoapySDR device through
the
.B hackrfduplex
module, as any SoapySDR application would, receives for a while, and
reports the rate achieved, any samples lost, the CPU used, the board and
module versions, the sensors, and what the receiver heard.
.PP
It transmits nothing unless
.BR \-\-tx\-tone ,
.B \-\-timed\-bursts
or
.B \-\-timed\-window
is given. Transmit only where that is allowed, with antennas or an
attenuated cable between the boards.
.PP
With
.B \-\-tx\-tone
it transmits a tone and reports its level and offset at the receiver,
which checks both boards in one run. With
.B \-\-timed\-bursts
it transmits a burst at each receive timestamp plus a delay and reports
where each one arrived against the time asked; every burst on time is
the module's timed-transmit guarantee. With
.B \-\-timed\-window
it opens a timed receive window around each burst instead.
.PP
The exit status is 1 when the pair cannot be opened or a stream ends in
an error, and, in the timed modes, unless every burst was found at the
sample asked for; otherwise 0. Overflows and underflows in a plain run
are reported, not counted against it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-rx\-serial " S"
The board that receives, by the end of its serial. Default: the lower
serial of two attached boards.
.TP
.BI \-\-tx\-serial " S"
The board that transmits.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " HZ"
Sample rate of both directions (default 8e6).
.TP
.BI \-\-tx\-rate " HZ"
Transmit sample rate, if not the same as
.BR \-\-rate .
.TP
.BI \-\-rx\-freq " HZ"
Receive centre frequency (default 868.3e6).
.TP
.BI \-\-tx\-freq " HZ"
Transmit centre frequency (default 868.3e6).
.TP
.BI \-\-rx\-gain " DB"
Overall receive gain (default 32).
.TP
.BI \-\-tx\-gain " DB"
Overall transmit gain (default 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-tx\-tone " HZ"
Transmit a tone this far from the transmit centre (default off).
.TP
.BI \-\-seconds " S"
How long to run (default 5).
.TP
.BI \-\-clkout " WHICH"
.BR off ,
.B rx
or
.BR tx :
which board drives 10 MHz from its CLKOUT into the other's CLKIN.
Default: leave the boards as they are. CLKOUT is off after every
power-up.
.TP
.BI \-\-tx\-format " F"
CS8, CS16, CF32 or CF64 for the transmit stream (default CF32).
.TP
.BI \-\-buffers " N"
The streams' ring depth in USB transfers (default: the module's, half a
second at the rate on receive and 15 on transmit).
.TP
.BI \-\-time\-origin " O"
.BR burst ,
.B trigger
or
.BR none :
how the streams get a common time (default: the module's,
.BR burst ,
a short calibration burst on the receive frequency).
.TP
.BI \-\-calibrations " N"
Once both streams run, calibrate N more times and report the origin each
time found, and their spread.
.TP
.BI \-\-timed\-bursts " US"
Transmit a 4096-sample burst at each receive timestamp plus US
microseconds and report where each arrived. Allow 5.5 USB transfers
(720896 samples: 90112 at 8 Msps). Transmits.
.TP
.B \-\-recover
With
.BR \-\-timed\-bursts :
when the time origin is lost, calibrate again and go on; the run passes
if every loss was recovered.
.TP
.BI \-\-progress " S"
With
.BR \-\-timed\-bursts :
print running totals every S seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-timed\-window " US"
Transmit a burst US microseconds after the time now and open a timed
receive window just around it, again and again, and report where in each
window it arrived. Transmits.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-analysis
Count the received samples and nothing more, so the CPU reported is the
module's.
.TP
.BI \-\-rx\-format " F"
CS8, CS16, CF32 or CF64 for the receive stream (default CF32); any but
CF32 implies
.BR \-\-no\-analysis .
.TP
.B \-\-direct
Receive through SoapySDR's direct buffer access (CS8 only).
.TP
.B \-\-dc\-removal
Remove the receiver's DC offset automatically (a module built with the
receive corrections).
.TP
.B \-\-iq\-balance
Correct the receiver's I/Q balance automatically (likewise).
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the options and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOAPY_SDR_PLUGIN_PATH
Where SoapySDR looks for modules first; point it at a build tree's
.I module
directory to check that build rather than the installed module.
.SH EXAMPLES
Receive for five seconds with the receiving board driving the reference:
.PP
.RS
hackrfduplex\-check \-\-clkout rx \-\-seconds 5
.RE
.PP
Both boards in one run, a tone at TX gain 0:
.PP
.RS
hackrfduplex\-check \-\-clkout rx \-\-tx\-tone 100e3 \-\-tx\-gain 0
.RE
.PP
Timed transmit for a minute at 8 Msps:
.PP
.RS
hackrfduplex\-check \-\-clkout rx \-\-timed\-bursts 90112 \-\-seconds 60
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR SoapySDRUtil (1),
.BR hackrf_info (1)
.PP
The usage guide and design notes:
.I docs/usage.md
and
.I docs/design.md
in the source.
